^he "GRiDNetwork Manager ERS""""Functional Description"
^bo^un2. Functional Description^un^bo

^bo2.1 GRiDNetwork Overview^bo

A GRiD Network comprises GRiD Servers, workstations, and communication links. Two types of communication links are supported - GRiDLink and PhoneLink. GRiDLink is a communication link that ties together a GRiD Server and upto eight workstations in a local area network. Physically, it is a multidropped SDLC line where the GRiD Server is the primary station, and the workstations are secondary stations. A GRiD Server can support multiple GRiDLinks.

PhoneLink enables users to utilize a GRiD Server's resources from remote areas. Physically, PhoneLink may employ either public telephone lines or leased (private, dedicated, point-to-point) lines. Over a PhoneLink, only one user may connect to a GRiD Server at a time. A GRiD Server can support multiple PhoneLink connections.

Users connect to the GRiD Server from workstations over GRiDLink or PhoneLink and access the server's resources. The type of communication link used for the connection is transparent to the user once connection is established between the user and GRiD Server. Direct connections between workstations are not supported.

Applications resident in a GRiD Server may sign on to another GRiD Server in exactly the same way that a user would, over PhoneLink. Currently, PhoneLink is the only means whereby GRiD Servers can communicate with each other.

When configuring communications on a GRiD Server, the system administrator must consider the following:

1.	How many GRiDLinks must be supported, and on which cards?

2.	How many PhoneLinks must be supported, and on which cards?

3.	Is the GRiD Server part of a network of GRiD Servers that communicate with each other? For example, it may be necessary for users of one GRiD Server to send mail to users of another GRiD Server.

4.	If the GRiD Server is part of a network of servers, what are the telephone numbers of the other GRiD Servers, and what baud rates do they support? How do I give my access information (telephone numbers, baud rates) to the administrators of the other GRiD Servers?

5.	Are there any special considerations? For example,
	(1) it may be desirable to establish a leased PhoneLink line to another GRiD Server for fast communications, or
	(2) it may be necessary to dedicate a PhoneLink communication port to a specific GRiD Server application so that the port is guaranteed to be always available to that application, or
	(3) it may seem desirable to establish certain PhoneLink ports as Answer Only for users to sign on, some other PhoneLink ports as Originate Only so that server applications don't have to contend with users for communication ports, and some other ports as Originate And Answer so that they are available to users for dialing in and also available to server applications for dialing out to other GRiD Servers when the ports are not in use, or
	(4) some PhoneLink ports may be connected to external telephone lines and some to internal extensions from which an external line can be accessed by dialing a special code (usually 9).

6.	Which of the remote GRiD Servers are on the public telephone network (also called ^bopublic network^bo), which of them are on my own internal telephone extensions (also called ^boprivate network^bo), and which of them are accessible only through leased lines (also called ^boleased network^bo)? Note that it is possible for a GRiD Server to be on more than one type of network at the same time.

Answering these questions results in the desrired configuration for the GRiD Server. Network Manager is the tool whereby the desired communication configuration for a GRiD Server can be put into effect.


^bo2.2 Network Manager concepts^bo

Network Manager provides a central framework for configuring a GRiD Network and controls allocation of the GRiD Server's resources to users or applications wishing to utilize the GRiD Server's communications capabilities. This is accomplished by maintaining a database of information about network configuration and keeping track of the current state of local resources.

Using Network Manager, the system administrator determines the number of GRiDLinks, PhoneLinks, which GRiD Server ports (also called system ports) support GRiDLink and which support PhoneLink, and also any special characteristics of PhoneLink (such as baud rates, switched vs leased, originate vs answer). Network Manager also enables the system administrator to define the GRiD Servers that are part of the network, and how they will be accessed (telephone numbers and baud rates). Additionally, Network Manager enables a Network Administrator to create and distribute directories of GRiD Servers that are part of the network to each GRiD Server system administrator - Network Manager is capable of incorporating within its database (also called configuration) and using the information from the directory with minimal effort on the part of the system administrator. The forms and menus that enable these functions are described in later sections. In this section, some key Network Manager concepts are described so that the system administrator.


^bo2.2.1 Accessing remote GRiD Servers^bo

In a network of GRiD Servers, it is necessary to provide each GRiD Server with information as to how the other GRiD Servers can be accessed. The access information for a remote GRiD Server on telephone networks (public or private) comprises the remote GRiD Server name, the type of network (public or private) the remote GRiD Server is on, the telephone numbers for the PhoneLink ports on the remote GRiD Server, and the respective baud rates for each telephone number. The access information for remote GRiD Servers on leased networks (i.e., GRiD Servers accessible via leased line(s)) comprises the system ports connected to the leased line(s) on the local GRiD Server. The procedure for specifying the access information for a GRiD Server supporting a leased line is a little different from that for GRiD Servers on telephone networks (public or private), as the reader will see.

For remote GRiD Servers on telephone networks (public or private), the system administrator specifies all the relevant information through forms and menus put forth by Network Manager. Network Manager accepts this information and automatically creates connections to the remote GRiD Server based on this information. A ^boconnection^bo over a telephone network (public or private) is a correspondence between a telephone number for a remote GRiD Server and a set of system ports on the local GRiD Server through which the remote GRiD Server may be dialed, using the telephone number for the connection. Note that each system port in this set of ports must be compatible with the telephone number for the connection: for example, it is not possible to dial a purely 2400 baud telephone number from a purely 1200 baud system port.

By default, the set of system ports for a connection is implicitly specified to be the set of all ports compatible with the telephone number for the connection - in this case, when the connection is used, Network Manager selects any compatible system port that is not in use for establishing the connection. Network Manager also permits the system administrator to assign a specific set of system ports to a connection - in this case, the system port used will be one that is not in use, from the specified set

For leased networks, there are no telephone numbers.


^bo2.3 Configuration Interface^bo

After connecting to Network Manager via RemoteAdministrator, the first form seen by the administrator is the password form:

		Enter Password:

This form is skipped if the administrator also happens to be the system administrator.

Next, the main menu of Network Manager is presented:

					Configure a communications server card
					Add a connection to a remote system
					Modify a connection to a remote system
		(CODE-E)	Erase a connection to a remote system
		(CODE-O)	Edit options
					Change password
		(CODE-T)	Print configuration, save configuration
		(CODE-Q)	Quit, save configuration changes
		(CODE-ESC)	Cancel, don't save changes

Note that the word 'connection' in the above form denotes a network link, and 'remote system' denotes any remote processor the network link connects to, including GRiD Servers.

^bo2.3.1 Configure a communications server card^bo

Selecting this command results in the following menu to be displayed:

	Select card to configure:

	Card2 (128K)
	Card3 (512k)
	etc.

The exact format of the above menu is TBD. After a card is selected for configuration, the form for card parameters is presented:

	Enter card parameters:

	Port0 protocol type:
	Port1 protocol type:
	Port2 protocol type:
	Port3 protocol type:

Choices for protocol types are the three protocol types supported and 'other'. For each port whose protocol type is Phonelink, an additional form is presented:

	Enter port^itx^it parameters:

	Phonelink Type:     [Originate, Answer, Originate and Answer]
	Line Type:			[Switched, Leased]
	Speed:				[choices are baud rates]
	Modem type:			[Smartmodem-1200,  Other]
	Phone number prefix:[a string]
	Dial mode:			[pulse, touch-tone]
	Dial type:			[manual, automatic]

If the line type is leased, there is no dialing involved in establishing connection: therefore phone number prefix, dial mode, and dial type are ignored. The phone number is prefixed with the phone number prefix - this is useful for getting an outside line in local switchboard operation.

If the protocol type specified is 'other', the port can be used by an application in a manner unknown to Network Manager. If all ports in a card have their protocol types set to 'other', the whole card is reserved for applications not involving Network Manager. ^itNote: In the first release, protocol types for all ports on a card must be set to 'other' in order for an application to use one or more ports on that card for non-networking purposes.^it

