^bo2.3.2 Add a connection to a remote system^bo

Selecting this command results in a series of forms being presented to the administrator. The first form asks the administrator to fill in the remote system name:
^ep
	Enter remote system name:

	Remote System name:	[Editable/choice field]

The choices are previously defined names (if any). Once a remote system is selected, a form for selecting the connection protocol type is presented:

	Select protocol type:

	Protocol type:	[GRiDLink Secondary (*), Phonelink, Other]

^itNote: In the first release, GRiDLink Secondary is not supported.^it

The next form depends on the protocol type assigned to the network link. For Phonelink the following form is presented:

	Enter connection parameters:

	Phone number:		[string]
	Alternate number1:	[string]
	Alternate number2:	[string]
	Disconnect after:	[1-255 minutes, default = 10]
	Wait for answer:	[seconds, default = 30]
	Cost (*):			[low, medium, high]
	Dedicated:			[yes, no]
	System port0:		[choice of system ports, none]
	System port1:		[choice of system ports, none]
	System port2:		[choice of system ports, none]
	System port3:		[choice of system ports, none]
	Connection name:	[Editable field]

Alternate number1 and Alternate number2 are alternate telephone numbers to be used if the main number is busy. Up to four system ports may be specified for a connection - the first unallocated port is selected. The connection name (or network link name) is set by the administrator (the default name is TBD). For GRiDLink Secondary (*) and Other, the following form is presented:

	Enter connection parameters:

	Dedicated:			[yes, no]
	System port0:		[choice of system ports]
	System port1:		[choice of system ports]
	System port2:		[choice of system ports]
	System port3:		[choice of system ports]
	Connection name:	[Editable choice field]

If the connection is dedicated, it will be allocated only to applications that ask for it by name. Cost for connections supporting GRiDLink Secondary is 'low'. Choices for system port are strings of the form 'Card^itx^it Line^ity^it'. Only those system ports that have been assigned the same protocol type as the connection (network link) are present among the choices.

^bo2.3.3 Modify a connection to a remote system^bo

This command presents a menu of connections:

	Select connection from:

	Connection1 to Remote system1
	Connection2 to Remote system2
	etc.

Once the connection is selected, the form for modifying connection parameters (see 2.3.2) is presented.

^bo2.3.4 Erase a connection to a remote system^bo

This command presents a menu of connections:

	Select connection from:

	Connection1 to Remote system1
	Connection2 to Remote system2
	etc.

The selected connection is erased from the configuration.

^bo2.3.5 Edit options^bo

The following options form is presented:

	Enter options:
	Exit after initializing network:	[Yes, No]
	High speed threshold (*):			[integer]
	Medium speed threshold (*):			[integer]

If 'Yes' is selected, Network Manager exits after starting up the network. This option should be set to 'Yes' only if there is no necessity to sign on to other GRiD Servers (^itsee open issues^it). Any baud rate value beolw the medium speed threshold is a low speed value.

^bo2.3.6 Change password^bo

This command lets the administrator change the administrator's password through suitable forms. The initial password (before GRiD Network is configured for the first time) is a null string.

^bo2.3.7 Print configuration, save configuration^bo

This command presents the administrator with the usual transfer menu:
^ep
	Select command from:
	Save configuration changes
	Print configuration

^bo2.3.8 Quit, save configuration changes^bo

This command saves any configuration changes and causes an exit from Network Manager.

^bo2.3.11 Cancel, don't save changes^bo

This command exits from Network Manager without saving configuration changes.

